  "summary": "Wool-stitched flowers blazing on black — Estonia's most intoxicating embroidery.",
  "description": "On Muhu island, Estonian women developed a wool embroidery unlike the restrained line-work of neighboring parishes: dense bouquets and tendrils in saturated pink, green, blue and orange stitched over black or white wool skirts, headcloths and stockings. Techniques like couching and satin-fill made petals almost painterly; patterns mixed older geometric guard-motifs with 19th-century floral naturalism picked up from trade goods. Muhu dress became the flamboyant emblem of Estonian national costume, and island hobby circles still teach the stitch canon today.",
  "culturalContext": "Muhu's blazing wool bouquets made the island Estonia's most flamboyant costume parish; embroidery knowledge passes in island hobby circles and the Estonian National Museum's canon, and Muhu dress now headlines every song festival and national celebration as Estonian identity's loudest thread.",
